# A hosts file used ONLY by the JDK's own resolver during tests, via -Djdk.net.hosts.file.
#
# It exists to make one specific mistake visible. The proxy must connect to the address its DNS resolution
# returned and classified — never to the hostname, which would resolve a second time inside connect() and
# reach a socket without ever reaching the classifier. That mutation is invisible if both paths fail: the
# connection is refused either way and the test cannot tell which resolver was consulted.
#
# So the name below is deliberately given two different answers. The controlled DNS server answers it with a
# global-unicast address that nothing listens on; the JDK answers it with loopback, where the test does listen.
# A proxy that connects by address reaches nothing. A proxy that connects by name reaches the listener, and the
# test counts the connection.
#
# This changes nothing about production behaviour: the property only affects java.net.InetAddress lookups,
# which the proxy performs exactly nowhere, and the address classifier never sees it.
127.0.0.1 exact-address.example.com
